Familial Mediterranean fever
Rheumatology & Immunology
Recurrent brief fevers, Sterile peritonitis/pleuritis, MEFV gene mutations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, spontaneous abdominal pain and high fever in a young patient, mimicking an acute surgical abdomen, requiring immediate evaluation to determine the necessity of emergency surgical transfer.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of agonizing abdominal pain, rigidity, and high fever. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to differentiate acute appendicitis or perforated viscus from an inflammatory peritonitis flare, as an incorrect triage could result in a fatal delay of surgical intervention or an unnecessary laparotomy. Waiting until the next daytime clinic was not clinically viable.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach hurts so much I can't breathe deeply or move, and I'm burning up with fever."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 12-hour history of acute, rapidly escalating, severe, spontaneous abdominal pain. The pain is diffuse, sharp, and intensely worsened by any movement or deep breathing. This was preceded by the sudden onset of a high fever (39.5 C) and rigors. Patient notes a known history of recurrent, brief episodes of high fever lasting 1 to 3 days since childhood, interspersed with complete asymptomatic intervals. Family history is highly significant for similar recurrent fevers in siblings of Mediterranean descent. Also reports mild right-sided unilateral pleuritic chest pain with deep inspiration today. Denies vomiting, diarrhea, or urinary symptoms.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound, lying perfectly still in a fetal position to minimize peritoneal irritation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety from the parents regarding the severity of the abdominal pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 39.2 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient looks acutely toxic, flushed, tachypneic, and lies perfectly motionless.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Board-like rigidity globally. Exquisite rebound tenderness and guarding in all four quadrants. Bowel sounds are absent. The presentation perfectly mimics acute appendicitis or a perforated viscus due to profound sterile peritonitis. Chest: Splinting on the right side during inspiration; faint pleural rub auscultated at the right base. Musculoskeletal: Left ankle is warm, swollen, and tender (acute monoarthritis). Dermatological: An erysipelas-like, hot, erythematous plaque is visible over the left lower leg/ankle. Diagnostic tetrad heavily supported clinically: recurrent brief fevers, severe sterile peritonitis/pleuritis, and a compelling family history predicting underlying bi-allelic MEFV gene mutations.
Environmental Observations: Safe home environment, but patient requires immediate acute care transfer.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and ibuprofen administered by parents with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Familial Mediterranean fever - Status: Exacerbated (Acute serositis/peritonitis flare)
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis / Perforated Viscus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of concurrent unilateral pleuritic pain, acute ankle arthritis, the classic erysipelas-like rash, and the defining historical pattern of recurrent, self-limiting identical attacks, though surgical review remains necessary to guarantee safety.

Acute Pancreatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of severe vomiting, no history of gallstones/alcohol abuse, and the presence of defining autoinflammatory skin and joint features.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Serositis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The highly episodic, brief nature of the fevers (1-3 days) with complete resolution between flares, and the classic Mediterranean family history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, hyper-inflammatory crisis characterized by high fever and profound, rigid peritonitis. While the history strongly suggests a Familial Mediterranean fever flare, the abdominal signs represent a hard surgical abdomen. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was unequivocally required to document the exact peritoneal findings, recognize the accompanying syndromic signs (pleuritis, rash, arthritis) that point to FMF, and manage the immediate transfer to the Emergency Department. Deferring this assessment carried an unacceptable risk of missing a genuine surgical catastrophe (e.g., concurrent appendicitis), validating the MBS 585-600 urgent triage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Despite the high likelihood of an FMF flare, the patient has an acute rigid abdomen and requires immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent surgical review, abdominal imaging (ultrasound/CT), and laboratory investigations to definitively exclude an acute surgical emergency. If confirmed as an FMF flare, they will require management with NSAIDs/colchicine and monitoring.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) enforcement: Ceasing all food and drink immediately; actively linking this to ensuring the stomach is completely empty in case emergency abdominal surgery is required tonight. Positional bracing: Keeping the knees bent and pulled toward the chest while waiting; demonstrating how this specific physical posture releases tension on the abdominal muscles, reducing the severe peritoneal pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; requires acute hospital admission.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

